+We examined whether the reported increase in Brazilian asthma mortality during 2014–2021 persisted when population age structure was held fixed. We first reproduced Brum et al.'s analysis and documented agreements and discrepancies with the published results. We then estimated national rates among people aged ≥7 years and rates within ages 5–34, standardising separately within each range. Post hoc sensitivity analyses examined alternative population projections, WHO weights, count models, selection rules and pandemic-period restrictions. These analyses distinguish the observed crude increase from the national and age-specific trends supported by the data.
 Methods
-Study design
-We conducted a secondary analysis of publicly available mortality data, reproducing the national asthma mortality analysis by Brum et al. for 2014–2021 [1] and examining two extensions: direct age standardisation of national mortality rates and mortality trends among individuals aged 5–34 years. The project documentation identifies these as the primary questions, with ages 7–34 years as a technical control and ages 35–59 years as an additional comparison. The Brazil 2014 population was selected as the primary standard. Reproduction was assessed against a tolerance of 2% for the total number of deaths and agreement in the direction and approximate magnitude of the original age-group trends. Count regression models, the WHO standard population and an alternative population projection were subsequently introduced as post hoc robustness analyses.
-Mortality data and case selection
-Mortality records for 2014–2021 were obtained from the Brazilian Mortality Information System (SIM) [5] on 1 October 2026. The original source URLs were no longer fully accessible, so all years were retrieved from the current official archives. Possible retrospective revisions of these data may affect exact reproduction.
-Deaths were selected when the underlying cause contained ICD-10 J45 or J46. Records containing the COVID-related codes B342 or U072, or the text “COVID-19”, in the cause-of-death lines were excluded. Age was calculated from birth and death dates as the elapsed number of days divided by 365.25. Records with invalid or missing birth dates were excluded from age-specific analyses.
-Duplicate records were identified using the birth and death dates, underlying cause and five available cause-of-death fields. This procedure uses the variables loaded by our pipeline but does not exactly reproduce the original full-record deduplication.
-To reproduce Brum et al., we retained deaths with calculated age greater than six years. The 5–34-year analysis instead used all age-valid records, including deaths at ages five and six.
-Population denominators
-The original population workbook was unavailable. We reconstructed denominators using IBGE national population projections by single year of age and both sexes combined. The 2018 revision was used in the primary analysis [6], and the 2024 revision, incorporating the 2022 Census, in sensitivity analyses [7]. The exact population series used by Brum et al. could not be recovered.
-Mortality rates and age standardisation
-For the reproduction of the national crude series, deaths above six years of age were divided by the total population, following the construction inferred from the published code [4]. We also calculated a version restricted to the population above six years. Original age groups were reproduced using their apparent numerator and denominator definitions, with unresolved denominator differences documented in the reproducibility audit.
-Direct age standardisation used single-year age-specific mortality rates and the corresponding population weights from Brazil in 2014. Ages were represented as completed years from 7 to 89, with ages 90 and above grouped together. Consequently, this analysis used completed ages of at least seven years, whereas the reproduction retained the original continuous-age threshold greater than six years. These definitions were kept distinct rather than treated as identical samples.
-Rates for ages 5–34, 7–34 and 35–59 years used deaths and population denominators restricted to the corresponding ages. Post hoc analyses also used the WHO World Standard Population [8].
-Statistical analysis
-The primary trend models followed those of the published analysis: Gaussian generalised linear regression for national annual rates and ordinary least squares for age-group rates. Eight annual observations were available for each series. Slopes were expressed as deaths per 100,000 population per year, with 95% confidence intervals calculated using the t distribution with six degrees of freedom. We also reported changes between 2014 and 2021. For comparison with Brum et al., we calculated the mean of annual relative differences from the 2014 rate, their reported change metric.
-Post hoc analyses used Poisson, quasi-Poisson and negative binomial regression [9], with population offsets. A national age-adjusted count model included calendar year and nine age bands. Overdispersion was assessed to guide interpretation of count-model uncertainty. In a further post hoc analysis, the change in the crude rate for completed ages of 7 years and older was split into an age-composition component and an age-specific-rate component with the symmetric Kitagawa decomposition, using single years of age and the 2018 projection. Because a comparison between two years depends on its endpoints, the decomposition was repeated for six pairs of years. No correction for multiple comparisons was applied; secondary estimates were interpreted descriptively.
-Software, data and code availability
-Analyses were performed in R 4.3.3 [10]. The analysis code, intermediate aggregate tables, generated results and reproduction audit are available at https://github.com/EduIntellect/asthma-mortality-brazil-reanalysis. Mortality and population data were obtained from public Brazilian sources.
-Use of artificial intelligence
-Generative AI tools, including Claude Code (Anthropic) and GitHub Copilot (GitHub), were used to generate and review analysis scripts and to examine analytical results. The authors wrote and finalised the manuscript.
-The figures were rendered programmatically from analytical outputs rather than generated as images by an AI image-generation tool.
-Ethics
-The study used publicly accessible SIM mortality data and aggregated population projections without direct contact with participants. No identifying personal information was included in the reported results.
+Study design and reference reproduction
+This secondary ecological analysis examined national underlying-cause asthma mortality in Brazil during 2014–2021. The corrected primary population comprised people aged ≥7 completed years. We analysed ages 5–34 separately and used the nested 7–34 group to assess sensitivity to ages five and six; 35–59 and ≥60 provided further comparisons. The historical analysis using continuous age >6 was reproduced separately. The primary analysis retained IBGE 2018 denominators and Brazil 2014 standard weights. WHO, IBGE 2024, count models, Kitagawa decomposition and alternative selection and periods provided sensitivity or descriptive analyses. We applied no multiple-testing correction.
+We reconciled the archived 12-page manuscript of 10 October 2026 with a Word copy of concordant content and repository commit 3884c868a07164710a2e358180b3034f9cdcf642. Official SIM archives recovered on 11 October 2026 reproduced the versioned selection counts and year-age aggregates exactly. Historical execution hashes were available for SIM 2020–2021 only, so byte identity with the historical 2014–2019 files could not be established. The accompanying provenance manifest records the reference PDF hash and all verified input hashes.
+Selection, COVID mentions and record identity
+We selected records whose underlying cause field, CAUSABAS, contained ICD-10 J45 or J46 [5]. To retain the historical search scope, the corrected COVID rule searched LINHAA, LINHAB, LINHAC, LINHAD and LINHAII for B342, U072, U071 or literal COVID-19. A match in any field excluded the record once. We logged each code frequency and overlapping combination separately. Sensitivities used the original rule without U071 or retained all records with COVID mentions.
+The historical deduplication key combined parsed death date, elapsed-days age, underlying cause and the five cause lines. It collapsed 2018 source rows 971828 and 971831. Inspection of all public fields found differences in occupation and seven administrative fields (NUMEROLOTE, TPPOS, DTCADASTRO, DTRECEBIM, DTRECORIGA, DIFDATA and CONTADOR). The SIM dictionary identifies NUMERODO as the death-declaration number and describes registration and receipt dates as administrative information [12]. NUMERODO is absent from these CSVs. The available fields therefore do not establish whether the rows refer to the same death. We retained both in the primary analysis and assessed their collapse under the historical key as a sensitivity.
+Calendar ages and analytical domains
+Completed age was the difference between death and birth years, reduced by one if death preceded that year's birthday. For births on 29 February, the anniversary in a non-leap year was 1 March. We added a leading zero to seven-digit day-month-year strings and required an eight-digit valid calendar date. Missing or uninterpretable dates and birth after death were excluded and logged separately. We applied no upper-age exclusion and grouped ages ≥90 into the IBGE 90+ interval.
+Calendar age defined the ≥7, 5–34, 7–34, 35–59 and ≥60 groups. One sensitivity used days/365.25, with its floor defining single-year bins. The historical reproduction retained the continuous >6 threshold as a separate population.
+Denominators and direct standardisation
+National single-year populations for both sexes came from IBGE's 2018 revision [6]; the 2024 revision following the 2022 Census provided a sensitivity [7]. We used the total-population block on sheet BR in the 2018 workbook and SIGLA = BR with SEXO = Ambos in the 2024 workbook. Both provide ages 0–89 and an open 90+ interval. Corrected crude rates matched each numerator to the same population age range. To reproduce the historical construction, we divided continuous-age >6 deaths by the entire population and separately by population ≥7. The latter comparator retains the small mismatch between its historical numerator and denominator.
+For age domain D and year t, we calculated the directly standardised rate as follows.
+ASR(t,D) = Σ[a∈D] w(a,D) × 100,000 × deaths(a,t) / population(a,t).
+w(a,D) = population(a,2014) / Σ[j∈D] population(j,2014), so Σ[a∈D] w(a,D) = 1.
+Weights were fixed across years and normalised separately within each age domain, including 5–34 and 7–34. Each denominator revision supplied its own Brazil 2014 standard population. Deaths and population at ≥90 shared one open interval. WHO standardisation used five-year bands [8], combining the 90–94, 95–99 and ≥100 weights for 90+. We allocated three fifths of the WHO 5–9 weight to 7–9 before normalisation, assuming a uniform distribution within that band. Missing death-count cells were assigned zero; population cells had to be complete and positive. We checked that Brazil-2014-standardised and aligned crude rates were equal in 2014.
+Trends, count models and sensitivity scenarios
+We fitted Gaussian-family GLMs to national crude and Brazil-standardised annual rates and ordinary least squares to age-group and WHO-standardised rates. The identity-link Gaussian trends are numerically equivalent to OLS. Slopes were expressed as deaths per 100,000 per year, with t intervals using n−2 residual degrees of freedom. We reported absolute and relative endpoint changes separately from annual slopes.
+Count models used log-link Poisson, quasi-Poisson and negative binomial families [9]. For unadjusted age domains, log E[Y(t)] = α + β × year(t) + log population(t). The national age-adjusted model was log E[Y(b,t)] = α + γ(b) + β × year(t) + log population(b,t). Its nine categorical bands were 7–14 (reference), 15–24, 25–34, 35–44, 45–54, 55–64, 65–74, 75–84 and 85+. Annual percentage change was APC = 100 × (exp β − 1), with intervals obtained by transforming β ± critical value × SE. Quasi-Poisson used the t distribution with model residual degrees of freedom; Poisson and negative binomial used normal Wald intervals.
+Quasi-Poisson scales Poisson coefficient variance by estimated dispersion. Negative binomial estimates the quadratic variance function μ + μ²/θ and can change coefficients as well as uncertainty. For every specification, we recorded Pearson dispersion, θ, mean-model convergence, iterations, errors and warnings. Large θ estimates with iteration warnings were interpreted as approaching the Poisson limit; these warnings prevent a claim of successful θ convergence or exact equivalence to Poisson.
+Each sensitivity changed one condition from the corrected primary analysis. We examined elapsed-days age, the original COVID rule, retention of all COVID mentions, the historical deduplication key, exclusion of 2020 and restriction to 2014–2019. The last two focused on 5–34 and 7–34 and retained actual calendar years as covariates. Excluding 2020 left seven observations and 5 trend residual degrees of freedom; restriction to 2014–2019 left six and 4. We evaluated both denominator revisions and both standards and reported every model regardless of statistical significance.
+Kitagawa accounting decomposition
+For the corrected ≥7 population, symmetric Kitagawa decomposition [11] partitioned crude differences into an age-composition term C and an age-specific-rate term R.
+C = Σ[a] (w(a,1) − w(a,0)) × (r(a,0) + r(a,1)) / 2.
+R = Σ[a] (r(a,1) − r(a,0)) × (w(a,0) + w(a,1)) / 2.
+Crude(1) − crude(0) = C + R, with r in deaths per 100,000 and each year's w normalised within ≥7.
+We used the same six endpoint pairs with IBGE 2018 and 2024, namely 2014–2021, 2014–2019, 2014–2020, 2015–2021, 2016–2021 and 2014–2018. We checked the identities at full precision before rounding each displayed component independently. This can produce a discrepancy in the last displayed digit. Shares above 100% indicate that a negative rate term offsets part of the composition term. The decomposition is descriptive and does not identify causal mechanisms.
+Software, reproducibility and ethics
+We used R 4.6.1 [10] for reproduction and revision; the reference reported R 4.3.3. The code records package versions, configurations, input hashes, warnings and independent checks, including numerical differences under the current environment. Public inputs, verified extraction code and separate scenario outputs support reproduction. Code and revision outputs are available at https://github.com/EduIntellect/asthma-mortality-brazil-reanalysis/tree/revision/amaury-review .
+The historical workflow used Claude Code and GitHub Copilot. This revision used Codex for code, analysis checking and editorial drafting. Figures were plotted directly from analytical results. Both authors' review and approval of the final work remain pending.
+The study used public SIM mortality records and aggregate IBGE projections, with no participant contact, and reports only aggregate results. Formal ethics approval or exemption has not been documented. The applicable institutional and journal wording requires author confirmation, as detailed in the accompanying checklist.
 Results
-Reproduction of the original analysis
-The SIM files for 2014–2021 contained 19,105 deaths with asthma as the underlying cause. After the COVID-19-related exclusion and removal of duplicates (57 records, 56 of them in 2020–2021) and exclusion of 15 deaths without a valid date of birth, the reproduction included 18,583 deaths above 6 years of age, against 18,584 reported by Brum et al. [1] (Supplementary Table S1). Of these, 68.2% occurred at 60 years or older, as in the original study.
-The crude national rate rose from 1.01 to 1.14 per 100,000 between 2014 and 2021 (+12.5%), with a maximum of 1.26 in 2020 (Supplementary Table S2). The linear trend was 0.025 per 100,000 per year (95% CI 0.004 to 0.046; p = 0.029; Table 1), against 0.03 (95% CI 0.01 to 0.04; p = 0.01) in the original paper. A negative binomial model gave an annual increase of 2.3% (95% CI 1.0 to 3.6), close to the 2.5% reported originally. The published annual rates were 1.8% to 5.4% higher than ours, with a gap that widened over the period, which points to a different population series; we could not identify it.
-For the age groups of the original analysis, our slopes agreed in sign and significance class with the published ones: no trend below 18 years (p = 0.85), an increase at 18–59 years (slope 0.021; p = 0.044) and a non-significant decrease at 60 years or older (slope −0.071; p = 0.14). The magnitude of the changes did not match exactly, and the largest difference, at 60 years or older, could not be reconciled (Supplementary Table S3). The reproduction thus met the criteria defined in the project documentation: total deaths within 2% and agreement in the sign of the age-group trends.
-National trend after age standardisation
-Using the same completed-age population of 7 years and older for both calculations, the crude rate increased from 1.123 to 1.258 per 100,000 between 2014 and 2021 (+12.0%), whereas the directly age-standardised rate decreased from 1.123 to 1.078 (−4.0%; Table 1; Figure 1). The estimated slope of the age-standardised series over all eight years was 0.0002 per 100,000 per year (95% CI −0.0213 to 0.0217; p = 0.983). Thus, the analysis did not support an increasing national age-standardised trend, although the confidence interval did not establish equivalence to a zero trend.
-In post hoc count models (Supplementary Table S4), the age-adjusted national trend was +0.00% per year with quasi-Poisson intervals (95% CI −1.12 to 1.14) and +0.9% (−0.2 to 2.1; p = 0.10) with a negative binomial model. With the 2024 population projection, the negative binomial estimate was +1.1% per year (95% CI 0.04 to 2.3; p = 0.043), the only one of six age-adjusted specifications whose interval excluded zero. The crude series was overdispersed (dispersion 5.9); its annual increase of 2.3% had p = 0.029 with quasi-Poisson and p < 0.001 with negative binomial intervals. Using the WHO standard population, the standardised rate changed by −4.2% (p = 0.99) with the 2018 projection and by −2.0% (p = 0.73) with the 2024 projection (Supplementary Table S5).
-Decomposition of the change in the crude rate
-The age-composition component accounted for 62% to 193% of the observed crude-rate increase across the six endpoint comparisons, while the component associated with age-specific mortality rates changed sign (Figure 2; exact values in Supplementary Table S6). For 2014–2021, the positive composition effect (+0.191 per 100,000) exceeded the net increase (+0.135) and was partly offset by changes in age-specific rates (−0.057). Approximately 95% of the composition component arose among individuals aged 60 years or older.
-Ages 5–34 years
-The rate at ages 5–34 years rose from 0.182 to 0.219 per 100,000 between 2014 and 2021 (+20.3%; slope 0.0080 per year, 95% CI 0.0023 to 0.0138; p = 0.014; Table 1). The group had 1,591 deaths over the period, between 172 and 236 per year, with the lowest rate in 2017 (0.175) and the highest in 2020 (0.244) (Supplementary Figure S1). The control group aged 7–34 years gave a similar result (+23.5%; p = 0.011; Supplementary Figure S1); it contains nearly all the deaths of the 5–34 group, so it shows that the estimate does not depend on ages 5 and 6 rather than replicating it independently.
-The increase persisted in post hoc analyses. Overdispersion was negligible (1.07), and the quasi-Poisson model gave an annual increase of 4.0% (95% CI 1.2 to 7.0; p = 0.013); with the 2024 projection it was 4.2% (p = 0.011). Standardised to the WHO population within the group, the rate rose by 16.9% (p = 0.020) with the 2018 projection and by 19.1% (p = 0.015) with the 2024 projection.
-At 35–59 years the evidence was inconclusive: the rate rose from 0.719 to 0.830 (+15.5%; p = 0.13), and the count models disagreed (p = 0.13 with quasi-Poisson and p = 0.036 with negative binomial).
+Historical reproduction and corrected selection
+The historical pipeline reproduced 19,105 underlying-cause candidates, 56 COVID exclusions, one reduced-key removal, 15 uninterpretable ages and 18,583 deaths at continuous age >6. Brum et al. reported 18,584 deaths [1]. All versioned selection counts, annual age aggregates and IBGE-2018 denominators matched exactly. Rate and trend differences were at floating-point scale. Negative binomial θ estimates differed slightly under the current environment in models approaching the Poisson limit, with no change in significance classifications. Supplementary Table S3 documents the unresolved original population series and age-group discrepancies.
+The eight official files contained 11,169,644 records, including 19,105 underlying-cause asthma records. Corrected COVID exclusions removed 57 records, comprising 27 in 2020 and 30 in 2021. No record was removed as a proven duplicate; 15 had non-interpretable dates and none had birth after death, leaving 19,033 age-valid records. The ≥7 domain contained 18,544 deaths, including 12,678 (68.4%) at ≥60. Calendar age changed the single-year bin of 23 candidates (23 retained), but none crossed the ≥7, 5–34, 7–34, 35–59 or ≥60 domain boundaries relative to floor(days/365.25). Ages above 90 were retained in the 90+ interval. The corrected total ≥7 equals the historical total because retaining one 2018 record and excluding the additional 2021 U071 record cancel in the total.
+National domain of completed ages ≥7
+For completed ages ≥7, the aligned crude series rose from 1.123 to 1.257 per 100,000 (+12.0%; absolute change +0.1343), with slope 0.0270 per year (95% CI 0.0030 to 0.0509; p = 0.033). Brazil-2014-standardised mortality changed from 1.123 to 1.077 per 100,000 (-4.1%; absolute change -0.0456), with slope 0.0002 per year (95% CI -0.0214 to 0.0217; p = 0.986). The endpoint comparison describes the difference between 2014 and 2021. The slope uses all eight annual observations, and its interval includes trends in both directions.
+With 2018 denominators, the age-adjusted national estimate was +0.00% per year (95% CI -1.13 to 1.14; p = 0.999) under quasi-Poisson and +0.93% per year (95% CI -0.20 to 2.06; p = 0.106) under negative binomial regression. With 2024 denominators, the negative binomial estimate was +1.14% per year (95% CI 0.03 to 2.26; p = 0.044), the only one of the six corrected national age-adjusted specifications whose interval excluded zero. The national Poisson Pearson dispersion was 3.11; different uncertainty and variance assumptions contribute to differences between families. All mean-model iterations converged. Some unadjusted age-group negative binomial fits reached the theta iteration limit. Supplementary Table S4 identifies these warnings; convergence of the mean model alone does not establish dispersion convergence.
+Composition of the crude-rate change at ≥7
+Across six pairs, the composition component was positive under both revisions. With IBGE 2018 it accounted for 61.5–193.8% of the net increase, compared with 55.0–153.5% under IBGE 2024. For 2014–2021, the 2018 net change of +0.134 per 100,000 comprised composition +0.191 and age-specific rates -0.057; 94.8% of composition arose at ≥60. The corresponding 2024 terms were +0.146, +0.170 and -0.025. Contributions above 100% mean that a negative rate component partly offset the positive composition component; these proportions describe the decomposition and do not estimate causal effects.
+Mortality within ages 5–34 and sensitivity analyses
+Ages 5–34 contributed 1,590 corrected deaths (172–236 annually). Their crude rate rose from 0.182 to 0.218 per 100,000 (+19.7%; absolute change +0.0358), with slope 0.0079 per year (95% CI 0.0021 to 0.0138; p = 0.016). Standardising within 5–34 to Brazil 2014 yielded 0.182 to 0.215 per 100,000 (+18.0%; absolute change +0.0328), with slope 0.0075 per year (95% CI 0.0018 to 0.0132; p = 0.018); WHO standardisation yielded 0.178 to 0.207 per 100,000 (+16.4%; absolute change +0.0291), with slope 0.0068 per year (95% CI 0.0014 to 0.0122; p = 0.022). Thus the crude absolute increase was 0.0358, and the within-group-standardised absolute increase was 0.0328 per 100,000. The nested 7–34 comparison gave 0.181 to 0.220 per 100,000 (+21.0%; absolute change +0.0381), with slope 0.0088 per year (95% CI 0.0026 to 0.0149; p = 0.013); it tests sensitivity to including ages five and six. The crude 5–34 quasi-Poisson estimate was +4.00% per year (95% CI 1.13 to 6.95; p = 0.014).
+Excluding 2020 while retaining calendar-year values, the within-5–34 Brazil-2014-standardised slope was 0.0058 (95% CI 0.0004 to 0.0112; p = 0.040). In 2014–2019 it was 0.0068 (95% CI -0.0023 to 0.0158; p = 0.107); the endpoint increase was 22.4%. For the 7–34 control, the respective standardised trend p values were 0.021 and 0.067. The primary standardised prepandemic interval therefore does not establish a positive annual trend. With WHO standardisation and IBGE 2018, excluding 2020 gives p = 0.055, with an interval including zero. In 2014–2019, count-model inference also differs. The crude 5–34 Poisson and negative binomial Wald intervals exclude zero; the quasi-Poisson t interval includes it; negative-binomial theta has an iteration warning. Supplementary Table S7 reports results for both revisions, both standards and all three count families.
+The original COVID rule removes 56 records; adding U071 removes one further record in 2021, within both young domains. Of 18 U071 mentions in 2020, all were already excluded; of 19 in 2021, 18 overlapped the original rule. Retaining all COVID mentions raises the primary ≥7 numerator from 18,544 to 18,601. Calendar-age, original-COVID, all-COVID-retained and historical-key sensitivities change one condition at a time (Supplementary Table S8). At 35–59 the crude slope remained imprecise (p = 0.132); uncertainty is not evidence of stability.
 Discussion
-We reproduced the crude increase in asthma mortality reported by Brum et al. for 2014–2021, with one death of difference in the total count and the same direction of the national and age-group trends. After direct age standardisation, however, the national age-standardised trend was not statistically distinguishable from zero: the standardised rate fell by 4.0% and its slope over the eight years was close to zero, with a confidence interval that admits small changes in either direction. In a decomposition of the crude change, shifts in the age composition of the population accounted for most of the increase, and in the 2014–2021 comparison for more than all of it. The rise in mortality at 5–34 years, by contrast, persisted across every specification we examined.
-These results do not contradict the crude series of Brum et al.; they qualify its interpretation. A rising crude rate measures a rising burden of deaths in the population, which is relevant for health services. It does not by itself show that the risk of dying from asthma at a given age has increased. Cardoso et al. reported an increase in the number of asthma deaths over 2013–2023 while noting that the overall rate per 100,000 inhabitants remained stable [2]. Together, these studies illustrate why trends in death counts and crude rates should be distinguished from age-adjusted trends. In our reanalysis, changes in population age composition accounted for much of the crude increase. The statement that asthma mortality “shows an increasing trend for the first time in the past decades” [1] is consistent with the crude-rate increase reported by Brum et al., but our analysis does not establish a comparable increase in national age-standardised mortality during 2014–2021.
-The Kitagawa decomposition gives this reading a quantitative form, but it is an accounting decomposition, not a causal one. It splits a difference between two years into a component due to the age distribution and a component due to age-specific rates; it does not identify why either changed. Because two-year comparisons depend on their endpoints, we repeated it for six pairs of years. The composition component was positive in all of them, and about 95% of it arose at 60 years or older, the age range whose share of the population grew from 13.3% to 16.3%. The age-specific component changed sign with the endpoints, and was largest in the comparison ending in 2020, the year with the highest rates. We therefore treat the eight-year standardised trend as the main evidence, with the decomposition describing contributions to the crude increase.
-The national age-adjusted result was not uniform across specifications. One of six count-model specifications, the negative binomial model with the 2024 population projection, estimated an increase of 1.1% per year whose interval excluded zero. The remaining specifications gave estimates between 0% and 0.9% per year with intervals that included zero. With eight annual observations, these data cannot establish that age-adjusted mortality was constant; the age-adjusted point estimates were generally smaller than the crude estimate, although the confidence intervals did not rule out increases of comparable magnitude under some specifications.
-The increase at ages 5–34 requires separate interpretation. The rate rose by about 20% between 2014 and 2021, and the estimate held with count models, with the WHO standard population and with the alternative projection. This age range was used in earlier Brazilian surveillance, in which Graudenz et al. reported a decline in age-standardised mortality from 1980 to 2014 [3]. Our primary estimate for this group is not standardised within the group, and the two studies differ in period and methods, so the comparison does not establish a change point in 2014. The finding rests on 172 to 236 deaths per year, and the highest rate occurred in 2020. It warrants monitoring with longer series rather than a firm conclusion about a reversal.
-The reproduction also revealed limits to exact comparison with published estimates. Our population denominators differed from those of the original study, so our annual national crude rates were 1.8% to 5.4% lower, and the magnitude of the change at 60 years or older could not be reconciled. The original code made it possible to identify these differences, but the exact population series could not be recovered. Reporting the denominators used, together with age-standardised rates alongside crude rates, would make national asthma surveillance comparable across studies and years.
-Several limitations remain. The design is ecological and descriptive. The count models, the WHO standard population, the alternative projection and the decomposition were added after the main analyses and should be read as robustness or descriptive analyses, and we made no adjustment for multiple comparisons. The SIM files may have been revised since the original analysis, our deduplication did not reproduce the original procedure exactly, and changes in death certification or coding during the period, including during the COVID-19 pandemic, cannot be excluded. The questions were defined in the project documentation, but we could not document their timing relative to the analyses independently.
-Overall, the increase in crude asthma mortality in Brazil between 2014 and 2021 is reproducible, but it was not accompanied by a comparable increase in age-standardised mortality, and changes in population age structure accounted for most of it. Mortality at 5–34 years did increase over the period. National surveillance should report age-standardised and age-specific trends together with crude rates, because in this case they lead to different conclusions about the direction of asthma mortality.
+The corrected analysis found an increase in aligned crude asthma mortality among people aged ≥7 between 2014 and 2021. With Brazil 2014 age weights held fixed, the endpoint change was negative and the slope across all eight years was close to zero. The interval was compatible with trends in either direction and does not demonstrate stability. The positive age-adjusted negative binomial estimate with IBGE 2024 shows that national trend inference also depends on the model and population revision.
+Age composition contributed positively to the crude change for every endpoint pair under both population revisions. In some pairs, the composition term exceeded the net increase because the age-specific-rate term was negative. The decomposition explains how population composition and rates combine arithmetically; it cannot identify effects of death certification, treatment access or the pandemic. Endpoint comparisons and slopes across all annual observations describe different aspects of the series.
+Both crude and standardised mortality within ages 5–34 increased over 2014–2021. Standardising within this range accounts for changes in its internal age distribution. The primary Brazil-standardised slope retained a positive interval after excluding 2020; the corresponding WHO sensitivity with IBGE 2018 included zero. The nested 7–34 analysis assesses how including ages five and six affects the estimate and does not provide independent replication.
+The shorter prepandemic primary estimate was imprecise and its interval included zero. Its positive endpoint contrast alone does not establish an annual trend over 2014–2019. The observed increase over 2014–2021 warrants continued surveillance, while differences in study periods and age definitions prevent identification of a change point or reversal of the earlier decline reported by Graudenz et al. [3].
+The selection audit shows why equal ≥7 totals need not represent the same records. Retaining one ambiguous 2018 record and excluding one additional U071 record in 2021 cancel in the total. Calendar age also changed single-year bins without changing membership in the five age domains, affecting the age distribution used in standardisation and count models. Separate sensitivities identify the effect of each alternative selection rule.
+The ecological design, short annual series, post hoc sensitivity analyses, unadjusted multiple comparisons and possible changes in death certification limit interpretation. Public fields cannot establish whether the 2018 pair represents one death. Historical execution hashes for SIM 2014–2019 and the population workbook used by Brum et al. remain unavailable. Exact reproduction of recorded counts and aggregates therefore cannot establish historical file identity.
+Negative binomial θ iteration warnings near the Poisson limit limit confidence in dispersion estimation. We retained every model and its warnings to make these limitations visible. Statistical intervals quantify model uncertainty; they do not capture uncertainty in record selection, certification or population reconstruction.
+These findings support reporting aligned crude, age-standardised and age-specific mortality together. Age composition accounted for much of the crude increase among people aged ≥7. The increase within 5–34 over 2014–2021 merits attention, with interpretation bounded by prepandemic uncertainty and sensitivity to model assumptions.
